# netsurf: the sibling scan reads :not() / :is() / :where() in a left compound (patch 427)
# Generated 2026-11-09
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 426)
#
# The top of r139's decline list (10 + 10): "page CSS needs conservative
# sibling check" and "class change may move a sibling selector" -- a sheet
# whose sibling rule had a FUNCTIONAL pseudo-class in its left compound
# (patch 413 kept declining those) was conservative as a whole: every
# class change and block insert on the page took the full rebuild.
# Logged in a scratch build: youtube's input:not(:checked) ~ ...,
# .x:not(:empty) ~ ..., .y:is(:checked,:disabled) + ...; archwiki's
# input:focus:not(:active) + ... and :not(.a .b) + ...; rte's
# .p:not(.shaka-hidden) + ...; wordpress.org's :where(.a) + :where(.b)
# and :is(.a .b .c) + ....
#
# An argument that is a comma list of compounds -- each the last of a
# descendant / child chain -- is read into the compound's subs, and each
# is a left compound of its own: a change can flip X:not(A) or X:is(A, B)
# only by flipping X or one of them, so the class check (m_old != m_new
# per left compound) and the attribute check over every left see it; the
# pseudo itself is ignored like a plain one (a broader match). A chain's
# ancestors are the right compound's ancestors too (the two are
# siblings): a change to one rebuilds a subtree holding both. A sibling
# combinator, :has() or an unreadable part inside still declines as 413.
# :empty in an argument reaches the :empty checks.
#
# Conservative sibling declines on youtube / archwiki / rte / wpblog /
# wpnews: 6 / 1 / 1 / 1 / 1 -> 0. Those batches mostly meet the next
# decline now (structural selectors, a non-plain new element).
#
